<!-- Writing Journal 1: Who Am I As a Writer? — ENGL 1010 SBA -->
<Vertical id="wj1_sba_root">

<!--
---
description: "Writing Journal 1: Who Am I As a Writer? The first journal in a first-year ENGL 1010 unit on writer identity. Professor Ellington assigns the journal; the student, unsure they count as a writer at all, is invited by Alma to the in-person Writers' Circle, where they meet Lee and Richard. The student watches Alma talk through her own Writer Board, then builds their own — a Frequently/Sometimes/Never inventory of what they write and how often, for school and outside it — works out why they do different kinds of writing, then browses the Circle's deck of eight writer-type cards in a carousel and chooses the one that fits (no LLM, and no type assigned by the tool), defending the choice with a story. Same three peers as Writing Journal 2."
category: writing_journal1
index: 94
lang: en-Latn-US
---
-->

<Cast cast="characters.cast" id="wj1_sba_part1"
      title="Writing Journal 1: Who Am I As a Writer?"
      launchable="true">
  <!-- print="no-chrome": the print pages live in a tab, so the tab strip would
       otherwise head every exported PDF. Screen behavior is unchanged. -->
  <Tabs id="wj1_sba_tabs" print="no-chrome">

    <!-- Tab 1: the Writers' Circle meeting, with the activity for each step on the right -->
    <Vertical id="wj1_circle_tab" title="The Circle">
      <!-- Flags the chat flips to unlock gated content:
             wj1_board_ready: the board view in the My Journal tab
             wj1_types_ready: the Writer Types tab -->
      <Hidden>
        <LineInput id="wj1_board_ready" />
        <LineInput id="wj1_types_ready" />
      </Hidden>

      <!-- Attention cues. The TA reported that students didn't notice the right pane
           changing and didn't know where to look, so every screen the chat opens gets
           a pulse (Flash) as it lands, and the screens with something to fill in also
           get the caret (FocusAction) — which scrolls the control into view for free.
           Reading screens get the pulse alone. The chat points at them in dialogue
           instead (the arrow lead-ins in the chatpeg) and gates on their Done control,
           and the caret is deliberately NOT parked on that Done: it's the thing you
           press after reading, not before.

           These live HERE, beside the flag holders, and NOT inside the screens. A
           Trigger's prevValue is persisted component state; a Trigger mounted inside a
           UseHistory screen is unmounted the moment the pane moves on, so it never
           observes its own expression go false and never re-fires. Mounted out here it
           stays alive for the whole session, so mode="each" means each visit.

           FocusAction aims at the specific control, not the screen, so that a later
           edit reordering a screen can't silently move the caret somewhere useless. -->

      <Trigger id="wj1_welcome_cue"        watch="@wj1_sidebar.value === 'wj1_welcome'"        mode="each">
        <Flash id="wj1_welcome_flash"        target="wj1_welcome" />
      </Trigger>

      <Trigger id="wj1_orientations_cue"   watch="@wj1_sidebar.value === 'wj1_orientations'"   mode="each">
        <Flash id="wj1_orientations_flash"   target="wj1_orientations" />
      </Trigger>

      <Trigger id="wj1_alma_board_cue"     watch="@wj1_sidebar.value === 'wj1_alma_board'"     mode="each">
        <Flash id="wj1_alma_board_flash"     target="wj1_alma_board" />
      </Trigger>

      <!-- The board's first control is the top-left radio of the FOR SCHOOL grid. -->
      <Trigger id="wj1_board_cue"          watch="@wj1_sidebar.value === 'wj1_board'"          mode="each">
        <Flash id="wj1_board_flash"          target="wj1_board_school" />
        <FocusAction id="wj1_board_focus"    target="wj1_board_school" />
      </Trigger>

      <Trigger id="wj1_board_noticing_cue" watch="@wj1_sidebar.value === 'wj1_board_noticing'" mode="each">
        <Flash id="wj1_board_noticing_flash" target="wj1_board_noticing_text" />
        <FocusAction id="wj1_board_noticing_focus" target="wj1_board_noticing_text" />
      </Trigger>

      <!-- Not the screen: the first focusable thing on Why I Write is the "Remind me
           what's on my board" collapsible's toggle. The first real question is the
           messaging dropdown. -->
      <Trigger id="wj1_why_cue"            watch="@wj1_sidebar.value === 'wj1_why'"            mode="each">
        <Flash id="wj1_why_flash"            target="wj1_why_messaging" />
        <FocusAction id="wj1_why_focus"      target="wj1_why_messaging" />
      </Trigger>

      <!-- The profile screen's first control is now the card deck itself: the student
           browses eight writer types and picks one, so the pulse belongs on the whole
           Carousel rather than on any single card's button (which card is on screen
           depends on where they left off). The caret lands on the deck's first arrow —
           wrap="true" keeps both arrows live at every position, so it never lands on a
           disabled control, and focusing it scrolls the deck into view. -->
      <Trigger id="wj1_profile_cue"        watch="@wj1_sidebar.value === 'wj1_profile'"        mode="each">
        <Flash id="wj1_profile_flash"        target="wj1_type_carousel" />
        <FocusAction id="wj1_profile_focus"  target="wj1_type_carousel" />
      </Trigger>

      <Trigger id="wj1_wrapup_cue"         watch="@wj1_sidebar.value === 'wj1_wrapup'"         mode="each">
        <Flash id="wj1_wrapup_flash"         target="wj1_open_print_btn" />
        <FocusAction id="wj1_wrapup_focus"   target="wj1_open_print_btn" />
      </Trigger>

      <!-- wj1_blank gets no cue on purpose: the point of that screen is that the pane
           has gone quiet, and there is nothing on it to look at. -->

      <SplitPanel id="wj1_sba_split" sizes="38, 62">
        <LeftPane>
          <!-- print="false" keeps the transcript out of the exported PDF; SplitPanel's
               print stylesheet stacks the panes, so otherwise the whole conversation
               lands in the submission. It has to sit on <Chat>, NOT on <LeftPane>:
               SplitPanel's parser consumes the <LeftPane> tag and keeps only its
               children, so attributes on the pane tag are silently dropped. -->
          <Chat id="wj1_chat" print="false"
                src="writing_journal1_dialogue_part1.chatpeg"
                height="460px" />
        </LeftPane>
        <RightPane>
          <Vertical id="wj1_current_activity" title="Current Activity">
            <UseHistory id="wj1_sidebar" initial="wj1_welcome" />
          </Vertical>
        </RightPane>
      </SplitPanel>
    </Vertical>

    <!-- Tab 2: the running record of the student's work -->
    <Vertical id="wj1_journal_tab" title="My Journal">
      <Use ref="wj1_artifact" />
    </Vertical>

    <!-- Tab 3: the people in the Circle -->
    <Vertical id="wj1_people_tab" title="The Group">
      <Use ref="wj1_circle_members" />
    </Vertical>

    <!-- Tab 4: print &amp; submit — the one page that gets exported. It is defined in the
         activity bank (wj1_print) and the chat also parks it in the right-hand pane at
         the end, so there are two doors to the same page and no duplicated state. Tabs
         hide inactive panels with display:none, which print inherits, so whichever door
         the student came through, the PDF carries exactly one copy. -->
    <Vertical id="wj1_print_tab" title="Print &amp; Submit">
      <Use ref="wj1_print" />
    </Vertical>

    <!-- Tab 5: reference — all eight writer types. Tabs can't appear conditionally, so the tab is
         always present but its CONTENT stays gated until the student reaches the profile step
         (wj1_types_ready is set from the chat, which also announces the tab in dialogue). -->
    <Vertical id="wj1_types_tab" title="Writer Types">
      <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_types_ready.value === 'go'">
        <Markdown>*You'll unlock the full set of writer types once the Circle brings out the deck — keep going in **The Circle**.*</Markdown>
        <Use ref="wj1_writer_types" />
      </IntakeGate>
    </Vertical>

  </Tabs>
</Cast>

<!-- ============================================================
     REFERENCED CONTENT BLOCKS
     ============================================================ -->

<!-- Who's in the Writers' Circle (reads the cast) -->
<TeamDirectory id="wj1_circle_members" title="Who's in the Writers' Circle"/>

<!-- Reference: all eight writer types, for the "Writer Types" tab -->
<Markdown id="wj1_writer_types">
## The Eight Writer Types

This is the deck you'll choose from at the end of the session — you take the card that sounds most like you.
Three of them are how Alma, Lee, and Richard describe themselves, and most writers are a mix, so read all eight
before you settle.

| | Writer type | What drives them | A growth edge |
|---|---|---|---|
| 🤝 | **Community Writer** *(Alma)* | Connection — writing to reach and stay close to people. | Take feedback without becoming dependent on it. |
| 🧭 | **Explorer** *(Lee)* | Curiosity — writing to find out what they think. | Decide when a piece needs to land somewhere. |
| 🔧 | **Technician** *(Richard)* | Getting it done, and getting it right. | The parts of writing beyond correctness. |
| 🌿 | **Hobbyist** | Personal fulfillment — writing for the enjoyment of it. | Protect the joy while pushing to grow. |
| 💭 | **Dreamer** | Expression — putting an inner life into words. | Finish and share some of it. |
| 🔁 | **Practitioner** | Steady practice — a regular, useful habit. | Let some of it become writing for its own sake. |
| 🏁 | **Finisher** | Completion — ending up with something real. | Treat finishing as a beginning, not a test. |
| 🎯 | **Pragmatist** | Audience and impact — reaching people. | Keep your own voice alongside the goal. |

*It's a shorthand, not a verdict — and where you are today isn't where you have to stay.*
</Markdown>

<!-- Pull in the running-record artifact -->
<Use ref="wj1_artifact" src="artifact.olx"/>

</Vertical>
